{"hash":"f8922bc0e1a392fa","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":91.922,"said":"Here is the sea. This shore is Prabhas, ‘the place of radiance’, and the Shiva Purana tells how it earned that name. Long, long ago, Daksha, one of the Prajapatis, the first fathers of creation, gave his twenty-seven daughters in marriage to Soma, the Moon. But the Moon loved only one of them, Rohini, and neglected all the rest. Their sorrow reached their father. Daksha warned his son-in-law once, and then again. When nothing changed, he cursed him: the Moon would waste away. Night after night his light grew thinner. The worried gods went to Brahma, and Brahma sent the fading Moon here, to this very shore, to seek Shiva. For six months Soma worshipped a linga made of earth and chanted the Mahamrityunjaya mantra, the great prayer that conquers death. At last Shiva appeared. He did not erase Daksha’s curse; he softened it. For one fortnight the Moon would wane, and in the next he would grow full again. That, the Purana says, is why the moon still waxes and wanes. And at the gods’ request, Shiva stayed here as Somanatha, Lord of the Moon. Now follow the sea wall. A pillar there points somewhere very far away.\n\nDid you know? Close by, the Hiran, Kapila and Saraswati rivers are said to meet the sea at the Triveni Sangam, where pilgrims take a holy dip.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1123},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1139,"end":1267}],"words":[[0.098,0.397,0,4],[0.507,0.12,5,7],[0.639,0.096,8,11],[0.747,0.361,12,15],[1.995,0.216,17,21],[2.224,0.312,22,27],[2.548,0.132,28,30],[2.692,0.601,31,38],[3.51,0.084,41,44],[3.606,0.361,45,50],[3.978,0.156,51,53],[4.147,0.577,54,62],[4.988,0.144,65,68],[5.144,0.096,69,72],[5.252,0.337,73,78],[5.601,0.469,79,85],[6.082,0.361,86,91],[6.454,0.264,92,95],[6.731,0.24,96,98],[6.983,0.216,99,105],[7.212,0.228,106,110],[7.452,0.397,111,115],[8.762,0.505,117,121],[9.447,0.385,123,127],[9.844,0.409,128,131],[10.373,0.565,133,139],